    "name",
    "group"
  ]
}

output:
 The requested object describes a playbook syntax definition. A playbook is a document that defines the exact set of steps required to successfully start and finish a given software release. The Release Engine uses playbooks to determine the actions it should take during a deployment.

Here is a valid example of a playbook:

```json
{
  "execution": {
    "hosts": [
      "server1",
      "server2"
    ],
    "steps": [
      {
        "notify": {
          "started": [
            "admin@example.com"
          ],
          "completed": [
            "admin@example.com"
          ],
          "failed": [
            "admin@example.com"
          ]
        }
      },
      "install_software"
    ],
    "description": "Install software on servers"
  },
  "group": "production",
  "name": "Install Software"
}
```

In this example, the playbook defines a single execution that installs software on two servers (server1 and server2). The execution includes a step that notifies the administrator at `admin@example.com` when the installation starts, completes, or fails. The playbook also includes a description of the execution.

